+
+## Current-run diagnostic evidence
+
+The supervisor replaces the mode-0600 `state/receiver-diagnostics.json` snapshot
+at each run start, after enrolling/validating the private profile and acquiring
+its exclusive receiver lock. A strict schema bounds its event tail to 64 entries
+and file size to 32 KiB. Only fixed event codes, elapsed times, supervisor PID,
+typed authentication booleans and recognized codec/rate/format values survive.
+Raw output, engine error suffixes, account/device identities, keys, session
+contents and song metadata are never retained. The `diagnostics` helper validates
+the private profile, directory identity, file type/owner/mode, size and full schema
+before emitting the snapshot; it follows no diagnostic-file symlink.
+
+The existing local health query records authentication independently of process
+and socket readiness. Log phrases such as “signed in” are diagnostic events and
+cannot assert authentication. The native window reads this snapshot without
+opening another engine connection, and only accepts the running supervisor PID.
+The first health check follows the 30-second startup grace; later checks occur
+every ten seconds. Snapshot reads occur every five seconds. Same-user processes
+and root remain trusted; these files are not a sandbox boundary against them.
+
+An observed FLAC codec plus sample rate is decoder evidence. Audible playback,
+source bit depth, hardware format and bit-perfect output require their own proof.
+Offline fixtures are `scripts/test-current-diagnostics.py` and
+`scripts/test-receiver.py`; they use synthetic lines and a private Unix socket,
+with no account, key, audio server or real profile access.
